            <div xml:id="d290">

                <pb facs="0434" n="340" sameAs="frus:frus1930v03/pg340"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">882.5048/224: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Liberia</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Carter</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Monrovia</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-02-06T13:00:00-00:44">February 6, 1930—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 1:43 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>19. My telegram No. 17, January 30, 1 p.m. Barclay<anchor n="17" xml:id="d290-f1"
                    /> informed me this morning that the Liberian Government had requested the
                    League to name successor to Meek but indicated that he doubted if the Commission
                    would have time to accomplish much unless it meets by March 10th. This last I am
                    inclined to regard as intended to lay the grounds for a reopening of discussion
                    re terms of reference and a consequent indefinite delay in the
                    investigation.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Carter</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d290-f1" n="17" xml:id="d290-f1-content">
                <p>Edwin Barclay, Liberian Secretary of State.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
